---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CloudflareOperatingDiagram from "../components/CloudflareOperatingDiagram.astro";
import { ArrowRight, Database, Globe2 } from "@lucide/astro";

const repository = "https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer";
const evidence = `${repository}/blob/main/docs/evidence/cloudflare-review-20261112/README.md`;
const baselime =
  "https://blog.cloudflare.com/80-percent-lower-cloud-cost-how-baselime-moved-from-aws-to-cloudflare/";
const workersPricing =
  "https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/pricing/";
const d1Pricing = "https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/platform/pricing/";
const inferencePricing = "https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/platform/pricing/";
const modelDocs = "https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/models";
const modelRetirements = "https://developers.cloudflare.com/changelog/post/2026-11-05-planned-model-deprecations/";
const inferenceWorkload = { attempts: 1_000, inputTokens: 2_000, outputTokens: 500 };
const models = [
  { label: "GLM-5.3", id: "@cf/zai-org/glm-5.3", docs: "glm-5.3", input: 1.40, output: 4.40 },
  { label: "DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017", id: "@cf/deepseek-ai/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017", docs: "deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017", input: 0.30, output: 1.20 },
];
const usd = new Intl.NumberFormat("en-US", { style: "currency", currency: "USD" });
const count = new Intl.NumberFormat("en-US");
const inferenceEstimate = (input: number, output: number) =>
  inferenceWorkload.attempts * (inferenceWorkload.inputTokens * input + inferenceWorkload.outputTokens * output) / 1_000_000;

const accessDocs =
  "https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/access-controls/applications/http-apps/";
const spendManagement =
  "https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/spend-management/";
const durableObjectsPricing =
  "https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/pricing/";
const analyticsEnginePricing =
  "https://developers.cloudflare.com/analytics/analytics-engine/pricing/";
const analyticsEngineLimits =
  "https://developers.cloudflare.com/analytics/analytics-engine/limits/";
const awsLambdaPricing = "https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/pricing/";
const awsWebFunctions = "https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/lambda-web-functions/";
const evidenceComparison = `${repository}/blob/main/docs/evidence/cloudflare-comparison-20261122/README.md`;
---
<Base
  title="Build your app, not your infrastructure"
  description="A Cloudflare-first operating model for App Composer: the resources the Creator Hub reference actually runs, its measured usage and included allowance, the operational work it removes and the limits we do not hide."
  ogType="article"
>
  <div class="cost-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Cloudflare-first, measured</p>
    <h1>Run your app on Cloudflare.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The supported Creator Hub reference runs on two Workers, two D1
      databases and one R2 bucket. There is no server to patch, no database
      host to run and no separate CDN to configure. The owner still controls
      the source, the data and every release decision.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/creator-hub">
        See the live reference <ArrowRight size={18} aria-hidden="true" />
      </a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/ready">Prepare your environment</a>
    </div>
    <p class="small">
      Observed 12 October–12 November 2026 in the operator’s Cloudflare
      account. This page separates a vendor estimate, our measured reference,
      a not-run comparison and illustrative growth assumptions. It is not a
      Cloudflare partnership, endorsement or official integration.
    </p>
  </div>

  <nav class="platform-index" aria-label="Cost and operations sections">
    <a href="#what-runs">What runs</a>
    <a href="#baselime">The Baselime precedent</a>
    <a href="#answer">Savings or simplification</a>
    <a href="#reference">Our measured reference</a>
    <a href="#growth">What changes with scale</a>
    <a href="#workers-ai">Workers AI cost guide</a>
    <a href="#operations">What you stop operating</a>
    <a href="#limits">Limits and claims</a>
  </nav>

  <section class="cost-section" id="what-runs">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The supported shape</p>
        <h2>The supported Creator Hub setup</h2>
      </div>
      <span class="evidence-label">One scenario · measured in production</span>
    </div>
    <p class="lede">
      The Creator Hub reference is two independently owned applications. Here /
      there receives and moderates contributions; Linkarooie curates the public
      profile and reads approved items through a service binding. Each app has
      its own Worker, source repository and database. Only Here / there stores
      uploaded media.
    </p>
    <CloudflareOperatingDiagram />
    <div class="fact-strip" aria-label="Reference resource counts">
      <div><strong>2</strong><span>Workers</span></div>
      <div><strong>2</strong><span>D1 databases</span></div>
      <div><strong>1</strong><span>R2 bucket</span></div>
      <div><strong>1</strong><span>service binding</span></div>
      <div><strong>1</strong><span>daily cron</span></div>
      <div><strong>2</strong><span>custom hostnames</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="small">
      There are no Queues, Workflows, Durable Objects or KV namespaces in this
      pair. The only scheduled work is Here / there’s existing daily
      maintenance trigger. Both apps are production deployments; the reference
      is operator-owned, not a customer-owned onboarding proof.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cost-section" id="baselime">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Externally sourced precedent</p>
        <h2>Cloudflare’s historical Baselime migration estimate.</h2>
      </div>
      <span class="evidence-label">Baselime · vendor estimate, 2024</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      Cloudflare’s 2024 Baselime case study describes a much larger
      observability workload moving from AWS to Cloudflare. The AWS figure is
      the old daily cost Cloudflare reported for that workload. The
      destination figure is Cloudflare’s own model for a comparable paying
      customer on Workers Paid, not a billed invoice. Neither is an
      independently audited saving, a like-for-like provider benchmark or an App
      Composer result. Baselime was acquired by Cloudflare in 2024.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Cloudflare’s 2024 estimate for Baselime’s daily infrastructure cost (USD)</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Workload</th><th scope="col">AWS before (reported)</th><th scope="col">Cloudflare after (modelled)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Compute</td><td>$650 Lambda</td><td>$25 Workers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>CDN</td><td>$140 CloudFront</td><td>$0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Streaming and analytics</td><td>$1,150 Kinesis / EC2</td><td>$300 Workers Analytics Engine</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Total daily</strong></td><td><strong>$1,940</strong></td><td><strong>$325</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Annualised</strong></td><td><strong>$708,100</strong></td><td><strong>$118,625</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="attribution">
      Difference by subtraction: roughly <strong>83.25% lower</strong>
      (1 − $325 ÷ $1,940), about <strong>$589,475 per year</strong> on the
      report’s 365-day annualisation. Cloudflare’s own post rounds this to 83%.
      Source:
      <a href={baselime}>Cloudflare’s Baselime case study</a>, published 31
      October 2024. The post does not isolate provider pricing from the rewrite,
      and it does not measure an optimised AWS counterfactual.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Do not read this as “83% cheaper with App Composer”. Baselime’s workload
      is not a WordPress site, a hobby app or a typical Creator Hub. Its value
      here is the architectural argument: fewer managed services can remove
      real operating work. Our own scenario is measured separately below.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cost-section" id="answer">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The overengineering objection</p>
        <h2>Did Cloudflare save the money, or did the redesign?</h2>
      </div>
      <span class="evidence-label">One estimate · contributors not apportioned</span>
    </div>
    <p class="lede">
      The honest answer is that the source does not say. Baselime’s move to
      Cloudflare changed the provider <em>and</em> rewrote the pipeline: fewer
      queues, no self-hosted ClickHouse, a different query engine and new
      service boundaries. A bill can fall for any of those reasons. The post
      reports a result for the combined change, so no share of the 83.25% can be
      assigned to Cloudflare’s pricing without a controlled comparison.
    </p>
    <div class="answer-grid">
      <div>
        <h3>What the source supports</h3>
        <p>
          The post reports about $1,940 a day on the AWS pipeline in 2024 and
          about $325 a day estimated after migrating. Both figures come from
          Cloudflare’s post, which names the workload and the services involved.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>What it cannot show</h3>
        <p>
          It cannot show that a simpler AWS design would have cost more, that
          Cloudflare caused most of the difference, or that a customer gets the
          same result. It also excludes the total cost of ownership and the
          migration effort.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>What would settle it</h3>
        <p>
          Three arms on one frozen workload: the owned baseline, a credible
          optimised AWS design and a Cloudflare-native design. Equal required
          behaviour first, then measured cost per successful outcome.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <details class="answer-detail">
      <summary>Read the technical explanation and FAQ</summary>
      <div class="answer-body">
        <h3>Why not just optimise AWS?</h3>
        <p>
          Optimising AWS is a real alternative, and the 2024 post does not test
          it. The old pipeline was genuinely expensive for a specific reason:
          Lambda Event Functions bill duration for the wall-clock time the code
          runs, and the receptors spent most of that time waiting on network I/O
          to the next hop. The post reports over 70% of wall time in I/O. A
          smaller number of hops, a cheaper queue, or AWS’s own newer execution
          models could reduce that bill without changing provider, and none of
          that counterfactual appears in the comparison.
        </p>
        <p>
          Current AWS options are not interchangeable. Lambda Event Functions
          charge for requests plus GB-seconds of allocated memory across the
          function’s duration. Lambda Web Functions charge for requests, active
          CPU time, the memory each execution environment consumes for as long
          as it runs, and data transfer. Workers Standard charges for requests
          and CPU milliseconds with a $5 monthly account minimum. Durable Objects
          add wall-clock duration while an object is active or cannot hibernate.
          The cheapest model depends on whether the workload waits on I/O or
          burns CPU, so “Cloudflare” and “AWS” are the wrong units of comparison.
        </p>

        <h3>Is the 2024 example still relevant?</h3>
        <p>
          As a lesson about service boundaries, yes. As a current price list, no.
          It describes one workload in one AWS region (eu-west-1) during a
          post-acquisition migration, estimated against Workers Paid. Several
          billing details have moved since: Workers has an account-wide spending
          limit that can interrupt every Worker, Durable Objects bill duration
          and storage separately, and Analytics Engine publishes prospective
          rates it is not currently billing. Re-check the applicable product,
          account and region before quoting any figure.
        </p>

        <h3>Is the functionality equivalent?</h3>
        <p>
          The post does not establish that. It describes internal changes to
          Workers Analytics Engine for high dimensionality, and an adaptive
          resolution scheme that stores data at 100% down to 0.0001% and picks a
          table per query, trading accuracy for speed. Those are Baselime’s and
          Cloudflare’s internal arrangements, not a published customer control.
        </p>
        <p>
          Public Analytics Engine documentation is narrower: three months of
          retention, up to 250 data points per Worker invocation, 16 KB of blobs
          per data point and a 96-byte index. It does not offer a documented
          exact-versus-sampled query switch, an accuracy guarantee or a general
          export path for arbitrary retention. Exactness, tolerance, retention
          and export have to be qualified per workload before the Baselime
          design is treated as reproducible.
        </p>

        <h3>What does the estimate leave out?</h3>
        <p>
          The post states its savings “do not include the total cost of
          ownership”, and that the migration took three engineers under three
          months. Those are the exclusions it actually names. Support and
          commitments, dual-running, egress and inter-region traffic, training,
          the revert path and continuing operations are not broken out, so the
          post does not say whether each is inside or outside the daily totals.
          A decision has to price those unknown components itself, not just the
          destination rate.
        </p>

        <h3>Which trade-offs decide the answer?</h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <caption>Trade-offs that change the outcome, and how to test them</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Trade-off</th><th scope="col">Why it moves the answer</th><th scope="col">What would test it</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Metering basis</td>
                <td>I/O-bound work is charged by wall-clock duration on Event Functions and by active CPU elsewhere, so the same traffic can favour either provider.</td>
                <td>Run one workload on both execution models and record active CPU, wall time and memory per successful request.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Query exactness</td>
                <td>Whether a sampled answer is cheaper or faster depends on the billing model and query design; it is not the same product as an exact one.</td>
                <td>A ground-truth query set with rare events, an agreed tolerance and a check that samples are disclosed.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Retention and export</td>
                <td>Storage cost and usefulness both depend on how long data is queryable and whether it can leave.</td>
                <td>Load the required retention window and perform a full export and restore.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>State and durability</td>
                <td>Durable Objects bill wall-clock duration while active; a separately managed state store has its own billing and failure modes, so the two are not interchangeable.</td>
                <td>Inject retries, duplicates, reordering and a restart, then check the accepted result survives.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Spend guardrails</td>
                <td>A Workers spending limit stops new invocations across the whole account and excludes other products; a per-invocation CPU cap is a different control.</td>
                <td>Configure the intended limit and verify what stops, what keeps serving and what still bills.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Migration and operations</td>
                <td>A rewrite, dual-running and on-call effort can outweigh a rate difference.</td>
                <td>Record hours, dual-run duration, training, revert path and continuing maintenance per month.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3>When should you stay where you are?</h3>
        <p>
          Stay when the current design already meets the requirement and the
          saving is unproven; simplify inside the current provider when the bill
          is dominated by avoidable hops, idle capacity or an expensive execution
          model; go hybrid when one component genuinely needs a different runtime
          or data location; migrate when a measured, like-for-like comparison
          clears the migration and continuing-operation cost; and answer
          “insufficient evidence” when the price, region, parity or usage data is
          missing. A vendor post, including this one, does not decide it.
        </p>

        <p class="small">
          <strong>Status of our own comparison:</strong> the bounded
          <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/issues/1022">#1022
          CLOUD-COST-01</a> suite compares an owned baseline, an optimised AWS
          design and a Cloudflare-native design on one frozen workload. It is
          <strong>not run</strong>: no suite exists yet, so there are no measured
          arms, no requirement-parity matrix and no savings figure. Until it
          runs, the Baselime estimate above stays an estimate and the Creator Hub
          readings stay measurements at small scale, not a Baselime-scale
          benchmark. Neither 83.25% nor a preferred provider is a benchmark
          target.
        </p>
        <p class="small">
          Primary sources checked 22 November 2026:
          <a href={baselime}>Cloudflare’s Baselime case study</a> (31 October
          2024),
          <a href={awsLambdaPricing}>AWS Lambda pricing</a>,
          <a href={awsWebFunctions}>Lambda Web Functions</a>,
          <a href={workersPricing}>Workers pricing</a>,
          <a href={spendManagement}>Workers spending limits</a>,
          <a href={durableObjectsPricing}>Durable Objects pricing</a>,
          <a href={analyticsEnginePricing}>Analytics Engine pricing</a> and its
          <a href={analyticsEngineLimits}>limits</a>. The dated table of
          publishers, publication dates, pricing basis and exclusions is the
          <a href={evidenceComparison}>comparison evidence brief</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="cost-section" id="reference">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">App Composer’s own observed result</p>
        <h2>Measured Creator Hub usage</h2>
      </div>
      <span class="evidence-label">12 Oct–12 Nov 2026 · 31 days</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      This is the supported two-app reference at
      <a href="https://creator-hub.loftwah.com">creator-hub.loftwah.com</a> and
      <a href="https://creator-hub-guestbook.loftwah.com">creator-hub-guestbook.loftwah.com</a>.
      The readings come from Cloudflare’s Workers and D1 analytics for the
      production scripts, plus the live D1 and R2 metadata. The source
      revisions served during the window were
      <code>b7cd3b3</code> for Linkarooie and <code>36797b8</code> for Here /
      there.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Observed reference usage and the applicable Cloudflare allowance</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">Observed</th><th scope="col">Included on Workers Paid</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Worker requests</td><td>1,660 across both apps</td><td>10,000,000 per month</td></tr>
          <tr><td>D1 rows read</td><td>2,841</td><td>25,000,000,000 per month</td></tr>
          <tr><td>D1 rows written</td><td>138</td><td>50,000,000 per month</td></tr>
          <tr><td>D1 stored data</td><td>249,856 bytes (about 0.25 MB)</td><td>5 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>R2 stored data</td><td>2 objects, about 0.12 MB</td><td>10 GB-month free</td></tr>
          <tr><td>R2 egress</td><td>Not separately measured</td><td>Free</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Scheduled invocations</td><td>One daily maintenance trigger</td><td>Billed as Worker requests</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="cost-callout">
      <Database size={24} aria-hidden="true" />
      <div>
        <h3>What this cost</h3>
        <p>
          At the observed request and storage volumes, the usage attributable
          to this pair is <strong>$0 above the account’s US$5 monthly Workers
          Paid minimum</strong>. We do not claim the pair costs $5 in
          isolation: the account also contains App Composer and other
          services, and Cloudflare bills the account minimum once.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="small">
      Excluded from this comparison: domain registration and renewal,
      Cloudflare plans beyond Workers Paid, Cloudflare Access / Zero Trust
      seats, agent or model usage, developer and maintenance labour, taxes,
      backups, support and every other resource in the account. A different
      app, traffic pattern or retention policy can change the result. The
      source data and commands are in the
      <a href={evidence}>review evidence</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cost-section" id="growth">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Assumption-labelled model</p>
        <h2>What changes as it grows.</h2>
      </div>
      <span class="evidence-label">Workers only · D1/R2 excluded</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      Cloudflare’s current Workers Paid plan includes 10 million requests and
      30 million CPU milliseconds per month. The rows below use Cloudflare’s
      own published examples for request and CPU charges. They do not include
      D1, R2, Access, domains or agent usage, so they are a lower bound rather
      than a whole-project quote.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Illustrative Workers charges under the current published price schedule</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Monthly assumption</th><th scope="col">Requests</th><th scope="col">CPU per request</th><th scope="col">Workers charge</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Observed reference</td><td>1,660</td><td>Not measured</td><td>$0 usage; $5 account minimum</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Small supported app</td><td>1,000,000</td><td>10 ms</td><td>$0 usage; $5 account minimum</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cloudflare’s 15M example</td><td>15,000,000</td><td>7 ms</td><td>$8.00 including the $5 minimum</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cloudflare’s 100M example</td><td>100,000,000</td><td>7 ms</td><td>$45.40 including the $5 minimum</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small">
      The 1M row is our assumption, not a measured run. The 15M and 100M rows
      reproduce Cloudflare’s published pricing examples. At higher volume, add
      D1 rows and storage, R2 operations and storage, Access seats and any
      other product you choose. A Workers spending limit can stop new
      invocations across every Worker in the account; it excludes the
      subscription fee, does not cover other products, and is not the same
      control as a per-invocation CPU limit. Current sources:
      <a href={workersPricing}>Workers pricing</a> and
      <a href={d1Pricing}>D1 pricing</a>, reviewed 22 November 2026.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cost-section" id="workers-ai" aria-labelledby="workers-ai-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Model inference</p>
        <h2 id="workers-ai-title">Estimate a text task on Workers AI</h2>
      </div>
      <span class="evidence-label">Published rates · 19 Nov 2026</span>
    </div>
    <p class="lede">
      Suppose your app extracts structured records from text. This estimate prices{" "}
      {count.format(inferenceWorkload.attempts)} attempts, each with{" "}
      {count.format(inferenceWorkload.inputTokens)} uncached input tokens and{" "}
      {count.format(inferenceWorkload.outputTokens)} billed output tokens.
      Both model versions require Workers Paid or prepaid AI Gateway credits.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Workers AI estimates">
      <table>
        <caption>Estimated inference at published rates (USD)</caption>
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col">Workers AI model</th>
          <th scope="col">Input per million tokens</th>
          <th scope="col">Output per million tokens</th>
          <th scope="col">Estimated total</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {models.map(model => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><a href={`${modelDocs}/${model.docs}/`}>{model.label}</a><br /><code>{model.id}</code></th>
              <td>{usd.format(model.input)}</td>
              <td>{usd.format(model.output)}</td>
              <td><strong>{usd.format(inferenceEstimate(model.input, model.output))}</strong></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Multiply billed input and output by their respective per-million rates and add the two amounts.
      Cloudflare hosts and meters these calls. Your coding agent uses its own provider or local runtime.
      AI Gateway can also route to external providers, with billing determined by the route and account setup.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      This is a list-price estimate before any allowance. It covers the stated attempts and excludes
      additional retries, plan fees, hosting, storage and tax. Quality, latency and cost per
      successful record need an actual run. See <a href={inferencePricing}>Cloudflare’s current Workers AI pricing</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Cloudflare lists GLM-5.3 Flash for retirement on 20 November 2026 and points to GLM-5.3 for
      frontier workloads. Check the <a href={modelRetirements}>Cloudflare model retirement notice</a>
      before choosing an endpoint.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button secondary" href="/media-studio#made">See real Workers AI artwork in Studio</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/benchmarks">Open Benchmarks Studio</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cost-section" id="operations">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The operating difference</p>
        <h2>What the owner and Cloudflare operate</h2>
      </div>
      <span class="evidence-label">Responsibility comparison</span>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Who performs each operating job in the two models</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Job</th><th scope="col">Typical self-managed stack</th><th scope="col">Creator Hub reference</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Runtime</td><td>Provision and patch a VM or container host</td><td>Cloudflare runs the Workers runtime</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Database</td><td>Run the database server, backups and failover</td><td>D1, with migrations committed to source</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Media</td><td>Bucket, CDN and credential rotation</td><td>R2 behind a Worker binding, free egress</td></tr>
